<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Volunteer Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a>–<a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Chinese People's Volunteers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a> was the force of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> that fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>. Sent into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in October 1950 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai">Peng Dehuai</a>, it drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> forces back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> and fought them to a stalemate before the armistice of July 1953, at the cost of hundreds of thousands of Chinese dead. The last units left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> in 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-volunteer-army.jpg" alt="People's Volunteer Army" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georgezh9617 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%AD%E5%9B%BD%E4%BA%BA%E6%B0%91%E5%BF%97%E6%84%BF%E5%86%9B%E8%83%B8%E7%AB%A0.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Every unit of the army was in fact transferred from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> on the orders of Chairman Mao Zedong, but it was constituted separately as volunteers so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> would avoid an official war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Preparations began soon after the war broke out: on 13 July 1950 the military command ordered the Thirteenth Army Group of the Fourth Field Army to become the Northeastern Border Defense Army, with Deng Hua coordinating. Chen Geng and Deng Hua acted as commander and commissar after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai">Peng Dehuai</a> fell ill in April 1952. About 3 million Chinese civilian and military personnel served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> during the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The army crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> in secret on 19 October 1950, the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> forces, and attacked on 25 October before withdrawing into the mountains. Its second offensive from 25 November overran several UN divisions at the Battle of the Ch'ongch'on River, destroyed Task Force Faith at the Chosin Reservoir and retook Pyongyang in December. Its forces and the North Koreans captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> on 4 January 1951, but the exhausted army was driven out of the city again on 16 March.</p><p>A spring offensive of about 700,000 men from 22 April 1951 was halted north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and in the east by 22 May. The front then settled roughly along the 38th parallel while talks went on from 10 July 1951 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a>, prolonged by the dispute over whether prisoners should be repatriated by force. The army relied on attacks on flanks and rear, infiltration and local superiority in numbers, and was armed mainly with captured Japanese and Nationalist weapons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Its dead are commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> at the Cemetery of the Fallen Soldiers of the Chinese People's Volunteers Army in Hoechang County. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the soldiers became known as the most beloved people, after an essay of 1951 by Wei Wei, and the war is widely regarded as an honourable struggle, the first in a century in which a Chinese army withstood a Western one. After the armistice 14,235 prisoners held by the UN were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, where they began arriving on 23 January 1954; that day became World Freedom Day in their honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Volunteer Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In October 1950 <b>Chinese People’s Volunteers</b> under Peng Dehuai entered the Korean War, drove United Nations forces back from the Yalu River and fought them to a stalemate before the armistice of July 1953, at a cost of hundreds of thousands of Chinese dead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Military</a></li></ul></section>
